==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|upblowen}}, equivalent to {{af|en|up-|blow}}.

===Verb===
{{en-verb|upblows|upblowing|upblew|upblown}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To [[inflate]].
#* '''1525''', uncredited translator, ''The noble experyence of the vertuous handy warke of surgeri'' by {{w|Brunschwig, Hieronymus}}, London, Chapter 48 “Of the wounde in the brest,”<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A03315.0001.001]</sup>
#*: {{...}} the pacyent hath heuynes and '''vpblowynge''' in the syde {{...}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|1|4|page=51|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A12777.0001.001|text=And by his side rode loathsome ''Gluttony,''<br>Deformed creature, on a filthie swyne,<br>His belly was '''vpblowne''' with luxury;}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1810|author={{w|George Crabbe}}|title={{w|The Borough (poem)|The Borough}}|section=Letter 16, p. 214|url=https://archive.org/details/boroughpoemintwe00crabiala
|passage=With Wine inflated, Man is all '''upblown''',<br>And feels a Power which he believes his own;}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To [[explode]], [[blow up]].
#* '''1666''', anonymous, Song 37, in Thomas Davidson, ''Cantus, songs and Fancies, to three, four, or five parts,'' Aberdeen,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A37220.0001.001]</sup>
#*: Ingyniers in the trench
#*: earth, earth uprearing,
#*: Gun-powder in the mynes,
#*: Pagans '''upblowing'''.
#* {{RQ:Hardy Dynasts|3|III|v|117|The bridge of Lindenau has been '''upblown'''!}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive|archaic}} To [[blow]] in an [[upward]] direction.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|3|4|page=447|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A12777.0001.001|text=The watry Southwinde from the seabord coste<br>'''Vpblowing''', doth disperse the vapour lo’ste,}}
#* '''1798''', {{w|Samuel Taylor Coleridge}}, ''{{w|The Rime of the Ancient Mariner}},'' part 5, in ''{{w|Lyrical Ballads}},'' London: J. & A. Arch, p.{{nbsp}}28,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/lyricalballadswi00word/page/28]</sup>
#*: The helmsman steerd, the ship mov’d on;
#*: Yet never a breeze '''up-blew''';
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1814|translator={{w|Henry Francis Cary}}|chapter={{w|Purgatorio|The Vision of Purgatory}}|title={{w|Divine Comedy|The Divine Comedy}}|author={{w|Dante Alighieri}}|section=Canto 25|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/8795/8795-h/8795-h.htm
|passage=Here the rocky precipice<br>Hurls forth redundant flames, and from the rim<br>A blast '''upblown''', with forcible rebuff<br>Driveth them back,}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1893|author={{w|Louise Imogen Guiney}}|chapter=Peter Rugg the Bostonian|title=A Roadside Harp,|location=Boston|publisher=Houghton Mifflin|page=3|url=https://archive.org/details/roadsideharpbook00guinuoft
|passage=The woods break down, the sand '''upblows'''<br>In blinding volleys warm;}}
#* '''1915''', {{w|Vance Thompson}}, “Swift Reversal to Barbarism” in ''Horrors and Atrocities of the Great War,'' L.T. Myers, p.{{nbsp}}105,<sup>[https://www.gutenberg.org/files/55503/55503-h/55503-h.htm]</sup>
#*: A blazing August sun; a road of pebbles and stinging, '''upblown''' dust.

